      "type": "function",
      "function": {
        "name": "get_adsorption_sites_text",
        "description": "JSON string containing classified adsorption sites with fractional coordinates.\n\nA JSON-formatted string containing a dictionary where keys are site types (e.g., \"top\", \"bridge\", \"hollow\") and values are lists of fractional coordinates for each site of that type.\n             Each coordinate is a list of three numbers [x, y, z] representing the fractional position within the unit cell.\n\nWhen to use: When to use this tool:\n    - Use when you need to identify all possible adsorption sites on a surface\n    - Best suited for systematic studies of surface reactivity and catalysis\n    - Essential for understanding how molecules interact with surfaces\n    - Recommended before placing adsorbates to understand binding options\n    - Avoid when you already know the specific binding site coordinates\n\nHow this tool works:\n    - Parses the slab CIF structure to identify surface atoms\n    - Uses AdsorbateSiteFinder from pymatgen to geometrically analyze the surface topology\n    - Classifies sites based on coordination environment (ontop, bridge, hollow)\n    - Calculates fractional coordinates for each potential binding site\n    - Returns sites organized by type in a JSON format for easy selection\n\nWorkflow: Typical workflow integration example:\n    1.  First obtain a slab structure using choose_slab_text or create_slab_from_structure_text \n    2.  Apply this tool to identify all adsorption sites on the surface \n    3.  Use choose_adsorption_site_text to select a specific site for adsorbate placement",
        "parameters": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "slab_cif": {
              "description": "CIF string of the surface slab structure. A properly formatted CIF string containing the surface slab structure with atomic positions, lattice parameters, and surface geometry. This should be a two-dimensional periodic structure with a well-defined surface and vacuum region. The structure is analyzed to identify potential adsorption sites.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "slab_cif"
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
